<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kenya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Kenya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kenya, officially the Republic of Kenya, is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, named after Mount Kenya. Its territory holds fossil evidence of early human evolution at Koobi Fora, and was later settled by Cushitic, Nilotic and Bantu speakers. European contact began in 1500 with the Portuguese. The modern country emerged from a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> established in 1895 and the Kenya Colony, which began in 1920. The Mau Mau revolution began in 1952, and Kenya's independence was declared in 1963. After independence Kenya remained a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a>, and it is now a presidential representative democratic republic.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 1700 the Ajuran state had disappeared, although Ajuran clans survive today in Somalia, Ethiopia and northern <b>Kenya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Mengistu wavered between vows of resistance and hints that he would kill himself like Emperor Tewodros II, and on 21 May 1991, saying he was going to inspect troops in the south, slipped into <b>Kenya</b> and flew with his family to Zimbabwe, which granted him asylum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The agreements of 1886 and 1890 between Berlin and London drew the line that became the border of <b>Kenya</b> and Tanzania, surveyed from the sea to Mount Kilimanjaro in 1892 and on to Lake Victoria twelve years later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Kurdish geographer and historian Abu al-Fida (1273–1331), who placed Malindi south of the mouth of a river rising in a mountain hundreds of miles away, perhaps Mount <b>Kenya</b>, where the Galana rises; the town has thus existed as a Swahili settlement since at least the thirteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malindi">Malindi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mombasa grew up on a coral island separated from the mainland of what is now <b>Kenya</b> by narrow creeks, which gave it one of the best natural harbours on the East African coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The caravan routes it financed carried Islam, the Swahili language, firearms, cloth and new crops deep into the interior, linking the peoples of present-day Tanzania, <b>Kenya</b>, Uganda, Malawi and the eastern Congo to the Indian Ocean economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
